comments.body:
The article could have shed more light on the role played by the shipowners in this tragic situation. It may not be Iranians who are not allowing ships to be evacuated.  It is the shipowners who have reasons not to do that.

Large merchant ships/tankers are like isolated villages. They need permanent residence to keep ship’s various systems - including its power plants, its washrooms and sewage system, its "restaurant and bar", its “post-office”, etc. - running. But:

1. Full evacuation requires shutting down systems that are essential to operating the ship.  Once a ship's systems are shut down, it takes time to bring them up and running again;

2. The owners are hoping the Strait would be open any day now. If their ships are not ready to move forward in the line-up once that happens, they could lose their turn to pass through. And;

3. Once a sailor goes shore, there is no guarantee that he will be back. Then the owners would be forced to hire new sailors, often at much higher salaries.

These days, thanks to highly automated control rooms, advanced navigation systems, and streamlined cargo-handling equipment, a typical modern tanker has a crew often less than 30 sailors. The weekly payroll of such a crew is typically in the $30,000 to $40,000 range. In contrast, the value of a merchant ship/tanker could easily exceed one billion dollars. That should explain why the owners would avoid risks of any kind to their ships and do not mind having the crew staying onboard for weeks.
